ing users, publishing, and a new analytics service are outside this task's authority. Source: [request.md](request.md), [evidence.md](evidence.md).\\n\\n**Status:** direction ready for planning a bounded improvement to import-status visibility. This exploration does not include an implementation plan. Benefit magnitude remains untested; no numerical success target is approved.\\n\\n## Decisions\\n\\n| ID | Question | State | Prerequisites | Authority / record |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| D1 | Which customer need should the first effort address? | Resolved: knowing import status | None | Delegated direction choice; D1 below |\\n| D2 | What direction should address that need? | Resolved: make existing job state visible on the import page | D1 | Delegated direction choice; D2 below |\\n| D3 | How can the result be assessed? | Resolved: comparable support sample; target still unapproved | D1 | Available support capability; D3 below |\\n| D4 | Should cancellation or date guidance change in this first effort? | Deferred from this selected direction; neither problem is solved | None | One-week scope choice and existing-semantics constraint; D4 below |\\n\\nD2 and D3 are related through the intended outcome; neither requires the other to be designed first.\\n\\n## D1: focus on import status\\n\\nObserved: 18 of 24 coded support conversations (75%) asked whether an import had finished; 4 concerned date columns and 2 cancellation. Median status-question handling time was six minutes. The import page shows only \u201cUpload accepted\u201d even though the API already exposes queued, running, failed, and completed states, plus a failure reason.\\n\\nThis is evidence of a visible information gap and a frequent problem in the supplied support sample. It is one week of support-contacting users, not all import users. The median does not establish total handling time or the savings a change will produce.\\n\\nDecision: address status uncertainty first. It has the strongest supplied evidence and a direct existing capability. The support lead's coding is supplied evidence; this session did not inspect raw conversations or conduct customer research.\\n\\n## D2: direction and alternatives\\n\\nChoose a clear, continuing job-state view in the existing import page, using existing authorized state and failure information. Customers should be able to distinguish accepted/queued, running, completed, and failed work. Preserve import behavior and permission boundaries.\\n\\nAn AI chat assistant is an alternative, but no experiment or customer research supports it here. It would still need to convey the existing state information. Select the direct status view for this one-week direction; keep chat as an unvalidated proposal rather than an approved architecture.\\n\\nThe support panel already reads these states without a new service. That supports feasibility of reuse, but does not establish a customer-facing design, update mechanism, service architecture, or measured support reduction. Those implementation choices remain for planning within the selected direction.\\n\\n## D3: evidence of benefit\\n\\nUse the support team's available comparable sample after the change, retaining the categories and measuring status-question count/share and handling time where available. Compare observation windows and import volume if available; otherwise state the traffic and sample limitations. A before/after sample can provide a directional signal, not prove causation.\\n\\nNo numerical target is invented. If a quantified release or success gate is required, its value needs approval; that does not prevent planning the bounded status improvement. The current evidence cannot support a promised percentage reduction or exact saved minutes.\\n\\n## D4: remaining areas\\n\\nDate guidance exists but its usage and effectiveness are unknown. Revisit if comparable evidence shows it is a material remaining source of support work. Cancellation is a consequential product question because running jobs cannot stop safely. Changing that behavior conflicts with the current constraint to preserve semantics; defer the choice and retain current behavior. Deferral does not establish feasibility or a permanent exclusion.\\n\\n## Planning handoff and next action\\n\\nPlan the status-visibility improvement within one week, reusing existing authorized information. Keep architecture open until relevant project details are inspected. Carry forward the sample limitations and the unapproved numerical target. Check customer-facing state/reason behavior and the unchanged permission boundaries during implementation verification; use the subsequent support sample to assess the intended benefit.\\n\\nUnspecified areas: exact presentation and update behavior, accessibility details, and the safety of existing failure-reason wording for customers need project evidence during planning. No evidence here establishes an architectural blocker, and this map does not approve a new service or chat system.\\n\\n**Continuity:** this is the authoritative map for this case. Resume from D2 and the planning handoff. It is stored in a disposable evaluation directory; preserve or transfer this map with request.md and evidence.md before that directory is cleaned. No external durable copy was created.\\n\",\"support-direction/response.md\":\"Choose an import-status view as the first direction. In the supplied sample, 18 of 24 support conversations asked whether an import had finished, while the API already provides the state that the import page lacks. There is no evidence yet that an AI chat assistant would improve this outcome.\\n\\nThe bounded status direction is ready for planning within the one-week constraint, preserving existing semantics and permissions. Architecture remains open, and support savings are untested. Use a comparable support sample afterward; no numerical success target has been approved.\\n\\nSaved the compact decision map to [decision-map.md](decision-map.md), including the reasoning, evidence limits, deferred date/cancellation questions, and next action. No product changes were made. The map is local to this disposable evaluation directory; preserve it with the supplied inputs before cleanup.\\n\",\"replay-evidence/decision-map.md\":\"# Offline note cache: decision map\\n\\n## Destination and current state\\n\\nChoose a recovery representation before implementation planning. **Not ready:** neither supplied candidate meets the approved correctness requirement and byte budget together.\\n\\nAuthority: the user approved D1 and the 32,768-byte large-fixture ceiling, authorized running the supplied candidates/probe and writing this map, and prohibited candidate changes and feature implementation. The user is offline. Do not treat silence as a budget change or authority to repair a candidate. Source: [request.md](request.md).\\n\\n## Decisions\\n\\n| ID | Question | State | Depends on | Owner / record |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| D1 | What must restart preserve? | Resolved: latest note values and deletions | None | User; request.md |\\n| D2 | What is the large-fixture artifact budget? | Resolved: at most 32,768 bytes | None | User; request.md |\\n| D3 | Which supplied candidate can meet both requirements? | Investigated: neither; representation choice remains open | D1, D2 | Agent observations below; user needed for expanded authority |\\n\\nThe previous small-case note said both candidates looked plausible. It is retained as historical evidence, not proof of the large-fixture requirements.\\n\\n## D3: experiment and observations\\n\\nBefore running the probes, the decision test was recorded in this map: on the large fixture, matches_expected must be true, deleted_note_reappears must be false, and artifact_bytes must be at most 32,768. Small-fixture checks are diagnostic only.\\n\\nExecuted the supplied [probe.py](probe.py) without modifications for each candidate and fixture. All four commands exited 0:\\n\\n| Candidate | Fixture | Bytes | Matches expected | Deleted note reappears | Raw command output |\\n| --- | --- | ---: | --- | --- | --- |\\n| snapshot | small | 278 | true | false | [snapshot-small.stdout.json](probe-results/snapshot-small.stdout.json) |\\n| updates | small | 20 | true | false | [updates-small.stdout.json](probe-results/updates-small.stdout.json) |\\n| snapshot | large | 108,028 | true | false | [snapshot-large.stdout.json](probe-results/snapshot-large.stdout.json) |\\n| updates | large | 20 | false | true | [updates-large.stdout.json](probe-results/updates-large.stdout.json) |\\n\\nCommands, run in this case directory:\\n\\n```text\\n/Users/luisurrutia/.local/share/mise/shims/python3 probe.py --candidate snapshot --size small\\n/Users/luisurrutia/.local/share/mise/shims/python3 probe.py --candidate updates --size small\\n/Users/luisurrutia/.local/share/mise/shims/python3 probe.py --candidate snapshot --size large\\n/Users/luisurrutia/.local/share/mise/shims/python3 probe.py --candidate updates --size large\\n```\\n\\nThe large snapshot preserves the tested latest values and deletion, but exceeds the approved byte ceiling by 75,260 bytes. Updates meets the byte ceiling, but resurrects deleted note \u201c1\u201d; it violates D1. Both reproduce the previous small-case plausibility, which does not survive the joint large-fixture test.\\n\\nSource inspection explains the distinction: the snapshot serializes the full expected state. Updates serializes only the changed value for note \u201c0\u201d and applies it over initial; it encodes no deletion. It also depends on that pre-existing baseline. Its small-fixture pass does not prove reconstruction from the 20-byte artifact alone.\\n\\nThese are synthetic replay observations, not production throughput, hardware-memory measurements, durable-write/crash tests, or evidence of production readiness.\\n\\n## Decision, implications, and next useful step\\n\\nDo not select either supplied candidate for a conforming implementation plan. This is a finding under existing constraints, not permission to abandon the initiative or weaken D1. A deleted-note failure cannot be accepted as a residual risk under the approved contract.\\n\\nThe unresolved question is: **what representation can preserve the complete required recovery state, including deletions, within 32,768 bytes on the large fixture?**\\n\\nA compact full-state representation or a baseline-plus-update/deletion representation could be investigated next; neither was implemented or tested here. Any required recovery baseline must be accounted for when judging whether the saved recovery artifact meets the contract.\\n\\nNext session: obtain permission to add or change a candidate, then rerun the joint large-fixture correctness-and-size test. Retain the existing requirements while doing so. Raising the budget is a separate user decision; it is not assumed or recommended as an automatic resolution. Current authority prohibits the candidate changes needed for that next experiment, so dependent implementation planning stays blocked.\\n\\nNo unformulated area needs expansion before this blocker. Production behavior remains outside the evidence supplied and would require later verification even after a candidate passes.\\n\\n**Continuity:** this is the authoritative map for this case. Resume at D3. This evaluation directory is disposable; preserve or transfer the map, request, unchanged probe, and raw outputs before cleanup. No external durable copy was created.\\n\",\"replay-evidence/response.md\":\"Neither supplied candidate is ready for implementation planning:\\n\\n- Snapshot restores the large fixture correctly but uses 108,028 bytes, exceeding the 32,768-byte budget.\\n- Updates uses 20 bytes but resurrects a deleted note, violating approved D1.\\n- Both passed the small fixture; those results do not establish large-fixture feasibility.\\n\\nAll four supplied probe commands exited 0. Raw outputs and exact commands are linked from [decision-map.md](decision-map.md). The candidates remain unchanged.\\n\\nThe unresolved question is which representation can satisfy both constraints. The next step needs permission to add or change a candidate; no budget increase or relaxation of deletion correctness was assumed while you were offline. The map and outputs are local to this disposable evaluation directory and must be preserved before cleanup.\\n\",\"changed-premise/decision-map.md\":\"# Archive migration\\n\\n## Destination and readiness\\n\\nMigrate stored archives while preserving the user-approved, mandatory 30-day cancellation period.\\n\\n**Status: not ready for execution planning.** The new vendor bulletin says undo tokens expire after 14 days. The previous token-based direction therefore cannot support the full approved cancellation window. Dependent execution planning is blocked until a compliant replacement is established.\\n\\nAuthority: the user authorized resuming and updating this map, with an execution plan only if the direction remains ready. Provisioning services and moving data are prohibited. The user did not authorize changing the cancellation period or reopening replication. Sources: [request.md](request.md), [evidence.md](evidence.md).\\n\\n## Decisions\\n\\n| ID | Question | Current state | Depends on | Owner / detail |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| D1 | What cancellation period must be preserved? | Resolved and unchanged: 30 days mandatory | None | User; D1 below |\\n| D2 | Can vendor undo tokens supply that cancellation guarantee? | Reconsidered: previous choice invalid under 14-day bulletin; replacement unresolved | D1 | D2 below, replacement question D4 |\\n| D3 | Is replication included? | User-excluded; feasibility never answered | None | User; D3 below |\\n| D4 | Can export/import preserve a complete recoverable archive for the full 30-day cancellation window with acceptable duration? | Open; missing archive-specific evidence and authorized test inputs | D1 | Investigation needed; D4 below |\\n\\nThe new bulletin invalidates D2's premise. It does not reopen D1 or answer D3. D4 is an alternative path prompted by the changed evidence; it does not depend on an answer to the excluded replication question.\\n\\n## D1: preserve 30-day cancellation\\n\\nThe user approved 30 days as mandatory in the original map and explicitly reaffirmed it in this request. Keep that contract. A 14-day token cannot silently replace it.\\n\\n## D2: preserve the old rationale and supersede its readiness claim\\n\\nPrevious decision: use a vendor undo token, relying on a bulletin promising 45-day validity. That gave the original direction a validity window longer than the mandatory 30 days.\\n\\nNew evidence: tokens now expire after 14 days. Under that current premise, token-only cancellation cannot cover days 15\u201330. Mark the old choice as needing a replacement; do not prepare an execution plan that assumes 45-day tokens. The previous rationale remains valid history, not current support for readiness. D4 records the next in-scope alternative question.\\n\\n## D3: replication remains excluded and uninvestigated\\n\\nThe user excluded replication to keep initial work small. The original \u201cclosed out of scope\u201d state did not establish feasibility, and the new bulletin supplies no benchmark. Preserve the exclusion; do not assume replication works, declare it impossible, or use it as an approved fallback. Reopening it would require a user-authorized scope change.\\n\\n## D4: investigate export/import feasibility before selecting it\\n\\nThe new bulletin establishes support for export/import, but says completeness and duration have not been measured for this archive set. Support for an API operation alone does not establish recoverability or fit with the mandatory cancellation window.\\n\\nThe next useful evidence must establish that a representative archive can be exported and restored completely, preserving required contents and properties; that a recoverable artifact remains available for cancellation throughout 30 days; and what export/import duration and operational limits are. Any consequential duration tolerance not already agreed needs a user decision once observed timings are available.\\n\\nNo such measurement, representative fixture, or validation access is supplied. Under this request, the agent cannot provision services or move data to obtain it. No export/import experiment or replication benchmark was run. Export/import is a candidate to investigate, not an approved replacement architecture.\\n\\nNext action: provide existing archive-specific completeness/duration evidence, or authorize a bounded disposable export/import test with a representative archive fixture and documented retention conditions. Define pass/fail evidence before that test: complete recovery, availability through day 30, and measured durations to compare against an agreed operational tolerance. Keep D1 and D3 unchanged during the investigation.\\n\\n## Direction for planning\\n\\nExecution planning remains blocked by D4. There is no authorized narrowing that makes a 14-day cancellation migration ready. Continue only the bounded evidence investigation once its missing inputs and authority are supplied; revisit readiness after D4 is resolved.\\n\\nOther operational details are not yet specified and should be developed only after a viable cancellation mechanism is established.\\n\\n## Original map, preserved verbatim\\n\\n> # Archive migration\\n>\\n> Destination: migrate stored archives while preserving 30-day cancellation.\\n>\\n> D1 resolved: user approved 30-day cancellation, mandatory.\\n> D2 resolved: use a vendor undo token. Based on the previous bulletin promising tokens valid 45 days; depends on D1.\\n> D3 closed out of scope: replication was not investigated; this is not an answered feasibility question. Excluded by the user to keep initial work small.\\n> Status: ready for planning under the 45-day premise.\\n> Next: prepare execution plan.\\n\\nThe original readiness statement is superseded by the current status above; original decision IDs are retained.\\n\\n**Continuity:** this is the authoritative map for this case. Resume at D4 without reopening D1 or D3. This evaluation directory is disposable; preserve or transfer this map with request.md and evidence.md before cleanup. No external durable copy was created.\\n\",\"changed-premise/response.md\":\"The direction is no longer ready, so I did not prepare an execution plan. Undo tokens now expire after 14 days and cannot support the mandatory 30-day cancellation period.\\n\\nUpdated [decision-map.md](decision-map.md) while preserving the original map and rationale. D1 remains mandatory; D2's token choice is invalid under the new premise; D3 remains excluded and uninvestigated.\\n\\nExport/import is supported but its archive-specific completeness, duration, and ability to sustain 30-day cancellation remain unproven. The next step is existing measurement evidence or an authorized disposable test with a representative archive fixture. No services were provisioned and no data was moved.\\n\\nThe map is local to this disposable evaluation directory; preserve it with its inputs before cleanup.\\n\"},\"commandRecords\":[{\"type\":\"command\",\"cmd\":\"/Users/luisurrutia/.local/share/mise/shims/python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nimport json, hashlib\\nroot=Path('.')\\nloading=json.loads((root/'loading.json').read_text())\\nactions=json.loads((root/'actions.json').read_text())\\npaths=['instructions/wayfinder/assets/decision-map.md','support-direction/request.md','support-direction/evidence.md','replay-evidence/request.md','replay-evidence/probe.py','changed-premise/decision-map.md','changed-premise/request.md','changed-premise/evidence.md']\\nfor name in paths:\\n raw=(root/name).read_bytes(); s=raw.decode(); digest=hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest()\\n bucket='supplied_instruction_resources' if name.startswith('